window.docContents[134].resolve({"/Equiv/#Hoare-Logic--Equiv___-Program-Equivalence--Behavioral-Equivalence--Definitions":{"contents":"\n\nWe'll define a type class Equiv, so that these relations (and\nthe one for commands, below) can all be written with the notation\n≃.\n\nclass Equiv (α : Type) where\n  equiv : α → α → Prop\n\ninfix:70 \" ≃ \" => Equiv.equiv -- you can type `≃` as \\simeq\n\ninstance : Equiv Aexp where\n  equiv := fun a₁ a₂ =>\n    ∀ (st : State), Aexp.eval st a₁ = Aexp.eval st a₂\n\ninstance : Equiv Bexp where\n  equiv := fun b₁ b₂ =>\n    ∀ (st : State), Bexp.eval st b₁ = Bexp.eval st b₂\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem Aexp.equiv_def {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} :\n    a₁ ≃ a₂ ↔ ∀ (st : State), a₁.eval st = a₂.eval st := by rfl\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem Bexp.equiv_def {b₁ b₂ : Bexp} :\n    b₁ ≃ b₂ ↔ ∀ (st : State), b₁.eval st = b₂.eval st := by rfl\n\n\n\n\nexample : aexp { X - X } ≃ aexp { 0 } := by simp\n\n\nexample : bexp { X - X = 0 } ≃ bexp { true } := by simp\n\n\n\n\ninstance : Equiv Com where\n  equiv := fun c₁ c₂ =>\n    ∀ {st st' : State},\n    (st =[ c₁ ]=> st') ↔ st =[ c₂ ]=> st'\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem Com.equiv_def {c₁ c₂ : Com} : c₁ ≃ c₂ ↔\n    ∀ {st st' : State},\n      (st =[ c₁ ]=> st') ↔ (st =[ c₂ ]=> st') := by rfl\n\n\nAre these two programs equivalent?X := 1;\nY := 2\nandY := 2;\nX := 1\n(A) Yes    (B) No    (C) Not sure\n\nWhat about these?X := 1;\nY := 2\nandX := 2;\nY := 1\n(A) Yes    (B) No    (C) Not sure\n\nWhat about these?while (1 ≤ X) {\n  X := X + 1\n}\nandwhile (2 ≤ X) {\n  X := X + 1\n}\n(A) Yes    (B) No    (C) Not sure\n\nThese?while (true) {\n  while (false) { X := X + 1 }\n}\nandwhile (false) {\n  while (true) { X := X + 1 }\n}\n(A) Yes    (B) No    (C) Not sure\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Equiv: Program Equivalence\u0009Behavioral Equivalence","header":"4.1.1. Definitions","id":"/Equiv/#Hoare-Logic--Equiv___-Program-Equivalence--Behavioral-Equivalence--Definitions"}});